{
  "case_id": "2008-07-751",
  "document_hash": "110043e615f1b81078154079f1e9f0c285a48a6036927a1945203014ad03e8b1",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2008-07-751",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "110043e615f1b81078154079f1e9f0c285a48a6036927a1945203014ad03e8b1",
      "evidence_id": "ev-110043e615f1-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 11:08 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2008-07-751",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "110043e615f1b81078154079f1e9f0c285a48a6036927a1945203014ad03e8b1",
      "evidence_id": "ev-110043e615f1-0000-0007",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Although the rule has become known as the doctrine of consent once removed, and the lower courts have banned various rationales in support of the rule, the name or label is not important."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2008-07-751",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "110043e615f1b81078154079f1e9f0c285a48a6036927a1945203014ad03e8b1",
      "evidence_id": "ev-110043e615f1-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: Well, because the Fourth Amendment principle of consent was well established? In other words, if a police officer goes to the door and says \"May I come in,\" that is -- that is perfectly acceptable. Is that -- that the level of generality that you think we ought to analyze this issue at?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Peter Stirba",
      "case_id": "2008-07-751",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "110043e615f1b81078154079f1e9f0c285a48a6036927a1945203014ad03e8b1",
      "evidence_id": "ev-110043e615f1-0000-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. STIRBA: Justice Souter, we are not -- we are not contending this is an implied consent case, although that has been a theory that has been advanced in the lower courts."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Malcolm L. Stewart",
      "case_id": "2008-07-751",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "110043e615f1b81078154079f1e9f0c285a48a6036927a1945203014ad03e8b1",
      "evidence_id": "ev-110043e615f1-0000-0005",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 18,
        "start": 18
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. STEWART: Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: The police entry in this case was constitutional and, in any event, did not infringe any constitutional right that was clearly established at the time the officers acted."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Souter",
      "case_id": "2008-07-751",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "110043e615f1b81078154079f1e9f0c285a48a6036927a1945203014ad03e8b1",
      "evidence_id": "ev-110043e615f1-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOUTER: But if our question is should consent once removed be recognized as a doctrine that covers the confidential informant in this case, one question that we may sensibly ask is, is there a need to recognize that broad a consent doctrine? And one question that would bear on that would be, in these cases, is there difficulty or impossibility of getting a warrant under normal probable cause standards? And I think that was the point of Justice Ginsburg's question."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondent",
      "case_id": "2008-07-751",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "110043e615f1b81078154079f1e9f0c285a48a6036927a1945203014ad03e8b1",
      "evidence_id": "ev-110043e615f1-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. STEWART: We certainly think that they had probable cause. There was testimony from police officers to the effect that magistrates in Utah would be reluctant to grant warrants based on these circumstances because of the possibility that the drugs would be dissipated."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2008-07-751",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "110043e615f1b81078154079f1e9f0c285a48a6036927a1945203014ad03e8b1",
      "evidence_id": "ev-110043e615f1-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: It is quite difficult. I mean there -- we don't know quite a lot about whether they would have gotten a warrant, how -- how dangerous it was, whether the drugs were likely to be hidden. And I was thinking of it, and that's why I am saying this, is that it's a perfect reason since constitutional questions in this area are -- are like the stars in the sky."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2008-07-751",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "110043e615f1b81078154079f1e9f0c285a48a6036927a1945203014ad03e8b1",
      "evidence_id": "ev-110043e615f1-0001-0004",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 28,
        "start": 28
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "ORAL ARGUMENT OF THEODORE P. METZLER, JR., ON BEHALF OF THE RESPONDENT"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Metzler",
      "case_id": "2008-07-751",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "110043e615f1b81078154079f1e9f0c285a48a6036927a1945203014ad03e8b1",
      "evidence_id": "ev-110043e615f1-0002-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. METZLER: Well, I think the officers in the Tenth Circuit need to be aware the way that our Federal court system works, and the Seventh Circuit decision isn't binding on the Tenth Circuit. And with all due respect to Judge Posner, he dropped ball on this one. He says this -- this is a the case where -- it's the United States v Paul -- there are no exigent circumstances; the officers there could have gotten a warrant and in fact should have gotten a warrant but he said it was justified by consent. He's saying that consent to one person is consent to many. And this Court's cases have said that consent is based on ordinary social expectation. When you let one person into your house, you just don't let in the whole world."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2008-07-751",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "110043e615f1b81078154079f1e9f0c285a48a6036927a1945203014ad03e8b1",
      "evidence_id": "ev-110043e615f1-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: If the Tenth Circuit says this is not allowed, and every other circuit since the Tenth Circuit's decision has held that it is, is that clearly established that it's not allowed in the 38"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Peter Stirba",
      "case_id": "2008-07-751",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "110043e615f1b81078154079f1e9f0c285a48a6036927a1945203014ad03e8b1",
      "evidence_id": "ev-110043e615f1-0002-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 53,
        "start": 53
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. STIRBA: The colloquy about the safety concerns when the officer or the confidential informer are in the home I think highlight why the distinction between a confidential informant and the officer really doesn't make much meaningful headway in terms of the Fourth Amendment."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2008-07-751",
      "confidence": 0.0,
